-- March8's core vocabulary (docs/MACHINE.md).
--
-- A name applies, as in FORTH, in a bracket too; a capitalized one is a
-- symbol, one lowercase letter a variable, and `name:` labels what follows.
-- A type is built by words: a type's name gives the type, and a
-- constructor builds one from types, `string i64 map`.
--
-- A definition is data: a name, what it names, and `;` (Thomas, 2026-10-12;
-- doc/design/SYMBOLIC-FORTH.md 18). A bracket before the body is the
-- clause's context: the types of its inputs, and after `--` the outputs it
-- promises. Clauses of one name are a family; applying it chooses the
-- clause whose inputs match best (doc/design/TYPES.md 2.8, 2.15). A type
-- defined is named. A test is applied by `?`, which a name with it asks for:
-- `positive?` is `positive ?`, and in a bracket it is a guard.
--
-- The core is the namespace `core` (doc/design/SYMBOLIC-FORTH.md 16): a
-- bare name defined here is the core's, `dup` being `core.dup`, which every
-- namespace finds last, as though it imported `core.*`. `io` and `case` are
-- namespaces of their own. A program adds a clause to a family here by its
-- path, `core.unit: …`, since its own `unit` would be its own.

-- How a literal becomes each type. A clause under a type's name is a
-- conversion: `!` by the type, `2 i64!`, converts a literal, and a clause
-- that wants the type converts a literal it is given (TYPES.md 2.10).

i64: < int# >. int#>i64 < i64 >. ;
f64: < int# >. int#>f64 < f64 >. ;
f64: < dec# >. dec#>f64 < f64 >. ;
money: < int# >. int#>money < money >. ;
money: < dec# >. dec#>money < money >. ;
dec#: < int# >. int#>dec# < dec# >. ;
bool: < int# >. int#>bool < bool >. ;

-- A ratio, `1:2`, is exact while it is a literal, and becomes an f64 rounded
-- once, or money if it is a whole number of cents. Its terms are kept as
-- written, so `12:30` is not `2:5`; computed, a ratio is in lowest terms. A
-- number is a ratio of two terms, the second not zero.

f64: < ratio# >. ratio#>f64 < f64 >. ;
money: < ratio# >. ratio#>money < money >. ;
ratio#: < int# >. int#>ratio# < ratio# >. ;
ratio#: < dec# >. dec#>ratio# < ratio# >. ;

-- A string converts to the number it writes, or to nil where it writes
-- none: parsing is converting (Thomas, 2026-10-13). `"42" i64!` is 42, and
-- `"x" i64!` nil, so the value is an `i64 nil or`.

i64: < string >. string>i64 < i64 nil or >. ;
f64: < string >. string>f64 < f64 nil or >. ;

-- An i64 converts to the f64 nearest it, `12 i64! f64!`. An f64 has no
-- conversion to i64: narrowing says how it rounds, `floor`, `round`.

f64: < i64 >. i64>f64 < f64 >. ;

-- A symbol is a value at run time too: its name, which a string converts
-- to and back. `"Open" sym!` is Open, and `Open string!` is "Open".

sym: < string >. string>sym < sym >. ;
string: < sym >. sym>string < string >. ;

-- Truth (Thomas, 2026-10-09): `bool` is 0 and 1, Iverson's brackets, and so
-- an i64 too: `mask +' reduce` counts. Comparisons leave one, and guards
-- take one. `not`, `and` and `or` are 1 - x, the minimum and the maximum,
-- which on values from 0 to 1 are also Zadeh's fuzzy connectives.

true: 1 bool! ;
false: 0 bool! ;
⊤: true ;
⊥: false ;

-- Classes: a union in a pattern matches any of its types, a little less
-- well than one of them alone (TYPES.md 3.6). `;` on a type names it.

num: < i64 f64 or money or int# or dec# or ratio# or > ;

-- Arithmetic. Two literals fold exactly, at compile time, and stay
-- literals; a literal beside a number takes its type. Money is added and
-- subtracted, not multiplied.

+: < int# int# >. int#+ < int# >. ;
+: < dec# dec# >. dec#+ < dec# >. ;
+: < ratio# ratio# >. ratio#+ < ratio# >. ;
+: < i64 i64 >. i64+ < i64 >. ;
+: < f64 f64 >. f64+ < f64 >. ;
+: < money money >. money+ < money >. ;

-- `-` is one value's negative, and `-+` subtracts, negating and adding in
-- one word until the compiler fuses the two (Thomas, 2026-10-09): `a b -+`
-- is a + (-b). So `-` has one meaning, as in APL.

-+: < int# int# >. int#- < int# >. ;
-+: < dec# dec# >. dec#- < dec# >. ;
-+: < ratio# ratio# >. ratio#- < ratio# >. ;
-+: < i64 i64 >. i64- < i64 >. ;
-+: < f64 f64 >. f64- < f64 >. ;
-+: < money money >. money- < money >. ;

-: < int# >. 0 swap -+ < int# >. ;
-: < dec# >. 0 swap -+ < dec# >. ;
-: < ratio# >. 0 swap -+ < ratio# >. ;
-: < i64 >. 0 swap -+ < i64 >. ;
-: < f64 >. -1.0 f64* < f64 >. ;
-: < money >. 0 swap -+ < money >. ;
-: < a ary >. [ - ] map ;

*: < int# int# >. int#* < int# >. ;
*: < dec# dec# >. dec#* < dec# >. ;
*: < ratio# ratio# >. ratio#* < ratio# >. ;
*: < i64 i64 >. i64* < i64 >. ;
*: < f64 f64 >. f64* < f64 >. ;

-- Arithmetic lifts over arrays: a tensor and an atom, a number, at any
-- rank, or a tensor and a row one rank lower, element by element along the
-- leading axis, recursively, so the lower broadcasts across the higher's
-- trailing axes (TYPES.md 2.8; Thomas, 2026-10-09: they are all tensors).
-- `atom` matches any type that is not a container. The other value is read
-- below each element; a literal takes the elements' type.

+: < a ary atom >. swap [ over + ] map swap drop ;
+: < atom a ary >. [ over + ] map swap drop ;
+: < a ary a >. swap [ over + ] map swap drop ;
+: < a a ary >. [ over + ] map swap drop ;

-+: < a ary atom >. swap [ over -+ ] map swap drop ;
-+: < atom a ary >. [ over swap -+ ] map swap drop ;
-+: < a ary a >. swap [ over -+ ] map swap drop ;
-+: < a a ary >. [ over swap -+ ] map swap drop ;

*: < a ary atom >. swap [ over * ] map swap drop ;
*: < atom a ary >. [ over * ] map swap drop ;
*: < a ary a >. swap [ over * ] map swap drop ;
*: < a a ary >. [ over * ] map swap drop ;

div: < a ary atom >. swap [ over div ] map swap drop ;
div: < atom a ary >. [ over swap div ] map swap drop ;
div: < a ary a >. swap [ over div ] map swap drop ;
div: < a a ary >. [ over swap div ] map swap drop ;

/*: < a ary atom >. swap [ over /* ] map swap drop ;
/*: < atom a ary >. [ over swap /* ] map swap drop ;
/*: < a ary a >. swap [ over /* ] map swap drop ;
/*: < a a ary >. [ over swap /* ] map swap drop ;

-- Two arrays, element by element (Thomas, 2026-10-09: as everything else
-- is), as long as each other: checked at compile time when both lengths are
-- known. `zip` pairs elements for any word.

+: < a ary a ary >. [ + ] zip ;
-+: < a ary a ary >. [ -+ ] zip ;
*: < a ary a ary >. [ * ] zip ;

-- `xs ys q table`: q on every pair, a row for each element of xs. `×` is the
-- outer product, the tensor product: each element of A times all of B, so
-- ranks add.

×: < atom b >. * ;
×: < a ary b >. swap [ over × ] map swap drop ;

-- `dot` contracts A's last index with B's first: vector · vector is a
-- number, matrix · vector a vector, matrix · matrix the matrix product.
-- `zip` checks that the contracted lengths agree, at compile time when both
-- are known. `transpose` swaps a matrix's two indices.

dot: < a ary b ary >. [ * ] zip +' reduce ;
dot: < a ary ary b ary >. swap [ over dot ] map swap drop ;
transpose: < a ary ary >. dup 1 at length range [ over [ over at ] map swap drop ] map swap drop ;

-- Multiplying is written `*`, which is typed; `⋅`, the dot operator, is its
-- glyph, as in algebra, and `×` is kept for the outer product (Thomas,
-- 2026-10-09: mathematics first, but `\cdot` at every product is too much).

⋅: * ;

-- Division. `/` is one over a value, its reciprocal, and `/*` divides, the
-- reciprocal and the product in one word until the compiler fuses the two
-- (Thomas, 2026-10-09): `a b /*` is a ⋅ 1/b, as `-` and `-+` are. `÷` is
-- `/`'s glyph. An integer has no reciprocal among the integers: its
-- division is `divmod`, floored, the quotient rounded down and the
-- remainder with the divisor's sign, so that a = q b + r and `-7 3
-- divmod` is -3 2. `div` and `mod` take one of them, so the three always
-- agree; `div` of floats is their quotient. Two literals divide exactly:
-- `1 3 /*` is the ratio 1:3, and `2 /` is 1:2.

divmod: < int# int# >. int#-divmod < int# int# >. ;
divmod: < i64 i64 >. i64-divmod < i64 i64 >. ;
div: < int# int# >. divmod drop < int# >. ;
div: < i64 i64 >. divmod drop < i64 >. ;
div: < f64 f64 >. f64/ < f64 >. ;
mod: < int# int# >. divmod swap drop < int# >. ;
mod: < i64 i64 >. divmod swap drop < i64 >. ;
div: < a ary a ary >. [ div ] zip ;

/: < ratio# >. 1 swap /* < ratio# >. ;
/: < f64 >. 1.0 swap f64/ < f64 >. ;
/: < a ary >. [ / ] map ;
/*: < ratio# ratio# >. ratio#/ < ratio# >. ;
/*: < f64 f64 >. f64/ < f64 >. ;
/*: < a ary a ary >. [ /* ] zip ;
÷: / ;

-- Comparisons leave a bool.

lt: < i64 i64 >. i64lt? < bool >. ;
lt: < f64 f64 >. f64lt? < bool >. ;
lt: < money money >. money-lt? < bool >. ;
gt: < i64 i64 >. swap i64lt? < bool >. ;
gt: < f64 f64 >. swap f64lt? < bool >. ;
gt: < money money >. swap money-lt? < bool >. ;
eq: < i64 i64 >. i64eq? < bool >. ;
eq: < f64 f64 >. f64eq? < bool >. ;
eq: < money money >. money-eq? < bool >. ;
eq: < string string >. string-eq? < bool >. ;
eq: < sym sym >. sym-eq? < bool >. ;

-- At most, at least, not equal: `≤`, `≥` and `≠`. "Less, or equal" rather
-- than "not greater", so that a float that is no number, NaN, is at most
-- nothing and at least nothing, but not equal to anything, itself included.

le: < a a >. over over lt? rot rot eq? or ;
ge: < a a >. over over gt? rot rot eq? or ;
neq: eq? not ;
≤: le? ;
≥: ge? ;
≠: neq? ;

-- Stack words. `=` is `dup` and `~` is `swap` (march7/docs/SURFACE.md,
-- on trial since 2026-10-03), and two wide, `=_2` and `~_2`.

=: dup ;
~: swap ;

-- `x q dip`, written `<>`, applies q with x set aside, and puts x back on
-- top: Joy's and Factor's `dip`, FORTH's `>r q r>` (Thomas, 2026-10-15).
-- With it the other stack words are words. `keep` applies q to x and keeps
-- x; `bi` applies two quotations to one value: `3 [ dup * ] [ 1 + ] bi` is
-- 9 4.

<>: dip ;
over: < a b >. [ dup ] dip swap < a b a >. ;
rot: < a b c >. [ swap ] dip swap < b c a >. ;
keep: over [ . ] dip ;
bi: [ keep ] dip . ;

-- A power and a subscript are rules of the core
-- (doc/design/SYMBOLIC-FORTH.md 21, 22, 24, 25), tried where nothing else
-- is found, as the core's names are. A rule rewrites a name it matches as
-- its captures, each a symbol, then its code. A symbol is global text, so
-- the code reads the word captured with `name#`, as a name where `dbl^2`
-- was applied (16): in `math` it is `math.dbl`, so `dbl^2` there is
-- `math.dbl 2 int# ! power# .`. `power#` takes a word's power as mathematics does
-- for its kind, and leaves a quotation: `sqrt^2` is a fourth root, `+^4`
-- the sum of four, `three^2` 9, and `+^0` the identity of `+`, below. A
-- clause of `core.power#` for a word and a count gives a meaning of its
-- own. A
-- subscript is an index, which a word's family gives a meaning, and where
-- none does, the core makes it a width of a word that moves values or is
-- handed code, each value moved a group of n: `=_2` and `dup_2` are
-- FORTH's 2DUP, `~_2` its 2SWAP, `dip_2`, `keep_2` and `bi_2` Factor's
-- 2dip, 2keep and 2bi, and a program's `tuck_2` follows from its `tuck`.
-- `width#` applies the word its symbol names, at that width; `+_2` is no
-- word, `+` neither moving values nor being handed code.

power#: < a b >. quote-power ;

/(.+)\^([0-9]+)/: [ name# ] dip int# ! power# . ;
/(.+)_([0-9]+)/: [ name# ] dip int# ! width# ;

-- Signs, as guards, and what they choose between. These clauses have no
-- `if`: a guard picks the clause (doc/design/TYPES.md 2.12).

zero: 0 eq? ;
positive: 0 gt? ;
negative: 0 lt? ;

abs: < a negative? >. - < a >. ;
abs: < a >. < a >. ;
min: < a a lt? >. drop < a >. ;
min: < a a >. swap drop < a >. ;
max: < a a gt? >. drop < a >. ;
max: < a a >. swap drop < a >. ;

not: < bool >. false eq? < bool >. ;
and: < bool bool >. min < bool >. ;
or: < bool bool >. max < bool >. ;

-- Narrowing: a float to an integer, saying how it rounds: down, up, half
-- away from zero, or toward zero. A float with no i64 is an error. On an
-- integer they change nothing, and on a decimal or a ratio literal they are
-- exact.

floor: < dec# >. dec#-floor < int# >. ;
floor: < ratio# >. ratio#-floor < int# >. ;
floor: < int# >. < int# >. ;
floor: < i64 >. < i64 >. ;
floor: < f64 >. f64-floor < i64 >. ;
ceil: < dec# >. dec#-ceil < int# >. ;
ceil: < ratio# >. ratio#-ceil < int# >. ;
ceil: < int# >. < int# >. ;
ceil: < i64 >. < i64 >. ;
ceil: < f64 >. f64-ceil < i64 >. ;
round: < dec# >. dec#-round < int# >. ;
round: < ratio# >. ratio#-round < int# >. ;
round: < int# >. < int# >. ;
round: < i64 >. < i64 >. ;
round: < f64 >. f64-round < i64 >. ;
trunc: < dec# >. dec#-trunc < int# >. ;
trunc: < ratio# >. ratio#-trunc < int# >. ;
trunc: < int# >. < int# >. ;
trunc: < i64 >. < i64 >. ;
trunc: < f64 >. f64-trunc < i64 >. ;

-- Roots and powers. `pow` is x to the y, written `^` as LaTeX writes it
-- (Thomas, 2026-10-09); `exp`, e to the x, is kept for that. Two literals
-- stay an exact literal.

sqrt: < f64 >. f64-sqrt < f64 >. ;
pow: < int# int# >. int#-pow < int# >. ;
pow: < i64 i64 >. i64-pow < i64 >. ;
pow: < f64 f64 >. f64-pow < f64 >. ;
^: pow ;

-- Containers, typed by their structure. A vec knows its length at compile
-- time, so its length is a constant.

length: < string >. string-length < i64 >. ;
length: < a ary >. ary-length < i64 >. ;
length: < n a vec >. vec-length < i64 >. ;
length: < tuple >. tuple-length < i64 >. ;
length: < k v map >. map-length < i64 >. ;

at: < string i64 >. string-at < i64 >. ;
at: < a ary i64 >. ary-at < a >. ;
at: < n a vec i64 >. vec-at < a >. ;
at: < k v map k >. map-at < v >. ;
at: < tuple i64 >. tuple-at ;
-- At an array of positions, an array: the element at each, as APL indexes.
at: < a ary i64 ary >. [ over swap at ] map swap drop ;

-- A map's value at a key, or nil where it has none: `v nil or`, decided at
-- run time unless the map and key are known (TYPES.md 3.6). Its outputs
-- name the union, so it is meant and not warned of.
get: < k v map k map-has? >. map-at < v nil or >. ;
get: < k v map k >. drop drop nil < v nil or >. ;

-- A map with a key set to a value: a new version, the old one unchanged.
put: < k v map k v >. map-put < k v map >. ;

concat: < string string >. string-concat < string >. ;
concat: < a ary a ary >. ary-concat < a ary >. ;
concat: < n a vec m a vec >. vec-concat ;

keys: < k v map >. map-keys < k ary >. ;
values: < k v map >. map-values < v ary >. ;

-- `sort`: an array in order, numbers by value and strings by text.
sort: < i64 ary >. i64-sort < i64 ary >. ;
sort: < f64 ary >. f64-sort < f64 ary >. ;
sort: < money ary >. money-sort < money ary >. ;
sort: < string ary >. string-sort < string ary >. ;

-- `order`, `<#`: the positions that would put an array in order, APL's
-- grade up `⍋` and R's `order` (Thomas, 2026-10-09: not "grade", too near
-- "gradient"), so that `xs dup order at` is `xs sort`; stable, equal
-- elements keeping their order. With `at` taking an array of positions,
-- `sort-by` sorts by any key: the keys made, ordered, and the array read in
-- their order (five words, long enough to keep). `dorder`, `#>`, is the
-- descending order, as APL's `⍒`, also stable, which `order reverse` is not:
-- that would turn equal elements around. The point of `<#` and `#>` is the
-- end where the smallest go.
order: < i64 ary >. i64-order < i64 ary >. ;
order: < f64 ary >. f64-order < i64 ary >. ;
order: < money ary >. money-order < i64 ary >. ;
order: < string ary >. string-order < i64 ary >. ;
<#: order ;
sort-by: over swap map order at ;

dorder: < i64 ary >. i64-dorder < i64 ary >. ;
dorder: < f64 ary >. f64-dorder < i64 ary >. ;
dorder: < money ary >. money-dorder < i64 ary >. ;
dorder: < string ary >. string-dorder < i64 ary >. ;
#>: dorder ;

-- `take`: an array's or a string's first n elements, or its last n when n is
-- negative; all of them, if it has fewer (from SYMBOL's `↑`).
take: < a ary string or i64 negative? >. - over length min over length dup rot -+ 1 + swap slice ;
take: < a ary string or i64 >. over length min 1 swap slice ;

-- `skip`: all but an array's or a string's first n elements, or all but its
-- last n when n is negative; none, if it has fewer (SYMBOL's `↓`; `drop` is
-- the stack's).
skip: < a ary string or i64 negative? >. - over length min over length swap -+ 1 swap slice ;
skip: < a ary string or i64 >. over length min 1 + over length slice ;

-- Failing (doc/design/SYMBOLIC-FORTH.md 29). A handler is a clause of the
-- word it handles, for its inputs and a trap on top, `k trap` of any kind
-- and `no-file' trap` of one. `kind` is a trap's kind, `why` its message,
-- or with none, its kind's name, and `fail` passes it on as it is.

kind: < k trap >. trap-kind < sym >. ;
why: < k trap [ trap-why "" eq? ] ? >. trap-kind < string >. ;
why: < k trap >. trap-why < string >. ;

-- Reading, in the core's first namespaces (TYPES.md 3.12): `io.stdin.read`
-- is standard input's text, and `"data.csv" io.file.read` a file's, when
-- the program runs, an effect; and read while compiling, `[ "notes.txt"
-- io.file.read ] !`, so that what follows can fold on its text, as a macro
-- would. Paths are from where march8 runs.

io.stdin.read: < >. stdin-read < string >. ;
io.file.read: < string >. file-read < string >. ;

-- Text: `split` at each separator (between characters, for ""), `lines`,
-- and `words` at runs of white space, as Haskell's are.
split: < string string >. string-split < string ary >. ;
lines: < string >. string-lines < string ary >. ;
words: < string >. string-words < string ary >. ;

-- `case`: a string in a case style (Thomas, 2026-10-09), each a word of
-- the namespace, so anyone may add one: `"Hello World" case.snake` is
-- "hello_world", and imported, `case.*` or `case.snake` alone, it is
-- `snake`. `lower` and `upper` go element by element over an array of
-- strings too.
case.lower: < string >. string-lower < string >. ;
case.upper: < string >. string-upper < string >. ;
case.lower: < string ary >. [ lower ] map ;
case.upper: < string ary >. [ upper ] map ;
case.snake: < string >. string-snake < string >. ;
case.kebab: < string >. string-kebab < string >. ;
case.camel: < string >. string-camel < string >. ;
case.pascal: < string >. string-pascal < string >. ;

-- `thru`: the integers from a to b, both included, counting down when a is
-- the larger (SYMBOL's `..`); `‥`, two dots as one character, is its glyph.
thru: < i64 i64 gt? >. over swap -+ 1 + range - swap 1 + + ;
thru: < i64 i64 >. over -+ 1 + range swap 1 -+ + ;
‥: thru ;

-- Sums and products: `∑` and `∏` (`\sum`, `\prod`), the mathematical
-- operators, not the Greek letters (SURFACE.md). An empty array's sum is 0
-- and its product 1; a mask's sum counts its truths.
sum: 0 [ + ] fold ;
prod: 1 [ * ] fold ;
∑: sum ;
∏: prod ;

-- `keep`: the elements whose mask is true, APL's compress.
keep: < a ary bool ary >. ary-keep < a ary >. ;

-- `count` is `length`, for now (Thomas, 2026-10-08: `#` may come later).
count: length ;

slice: < string i64 i64 >. string-slice < string >. ;
slice: < a ary i64 i64 >. ary-slice < a ary >. ;

-- Elements count from 1, and from -1 at the end; gaps between them count
-- from 0, before the first, each gap after its element, so -1 is after the
-- last (Thomas, 2026-10-09). `slice` includes both its ends.

first: < a ary >. 1 at < a >. ;
last: < a ary >. -1 at < a >. ;
rest: < a ary >. 2 -1 slice < a ary >. ;
most: < a ary >. 1 -2 slice < a ary >. ;

-- `xs v k insert` puts v in the gap after element k: 0 prepends and -1
-- appends. `xs k remove` takes element k out.

insert: ary-insert ;
remove: ary-remove ;

-- A quotation is a sequence too, while compiling (SYMBOLIC-FORTH.md 6):
-- its elements are what Low Stack holds, each a symbol or a quotation, so
-- `[ swap + ]`, which is `[| swap . + . |]`, has four. Run, a quotation's
-- symbols land on the stack and its meta-ops act; taken apart, nothing
-- runs, so a meta-op is held as the symbol of its name, `'.'`, and put
-- back, acts. Its names were expanded where it was written, so a name
-- taken out is its path, `math.dbl'`.

length: < quote >. quote-length < i64 >. ;
at: < quote i64 >. quote-at ;
slice: < quote i64 i64 >. quote-slice < quote >. ;
concat: < quote quote >. quote-concat < quote >. ;
insert: < quote b i64 >. quote-insert < quote >. ;
first: < quote >. 1 at ;
last: < quote >. -1 at ;
rest: < quote >. 2 -1 slice < quote >. ;
most: < quote >. 1 -2 slice < quote >. ;

-- A symbol is never a quotation, so the elements of code compare.

eq: < sym quote >. drop drop false < bool >. ;
eq: < quote sym >. drop drop false < bool >. ;

-- Identities (doc/design/SYMBOLIC-FORTH.md 21). x⁰ is the identity of x's
-- operation, the value that leaves the other operand as it is: `+^0` is
-- `+' unit# !`, a literal whose type comes where it is used, as `0`'s
-- does, and a value's `three^0` is `*' unit# !`, its power being its
-- copies multiplied. The facts are `unit`'s, the identity of each
-- primitive operation, and `least`'s and `greatest`'s, the ends of each
-- type's order. A program adds one by its path, `core.unit: < int#-' >.
-- drop 0 ;`.

unit: < int#+' >. drop 0 ;
unit: < dec#+' >. drop 0 dec#! ;
unit: < ratio#+' >. drop 0 ratio#! ;
unit: < i64+' >. drop 0 i64! ;
unit: < f64+' >. drop 0 f64! ;
unit: < money+' >. drop 0 money! ;
unit: < int#*' >. drop 1 ;
unit: < dec#*' >. drop 1 dec#! ;
unit: < ratio#*' >. drop 1 ratio#! ;
unit: < i64*' >. drop 1 i64! ;
unit: < f64*' >. drop 1 f64! ;
unit: < string-concat' >. drop "" ;

least: < i64' >. drop -9223372036854775808 i64! ;
greatest: < i64' >. drop 9223372036854775807 i64! ;
least: < f64' >. drop -1.0 0.0 f64/ ;
greatest: < f64' >. drop 1.0 0.0 f64/ ;
least: < bool' >. drop false ;
greatest: < bool' >. drop true ;

-- `x f stated#`: whether the fact f states one for x, `i64+' unit'
-- stated#` being true.

stated#: swap [ ] swap 0 insert clauses# length 0 eq? not ;

-- Whether code only moves values, by `dup`, `drop` and `swap`, and
-- whether it so picks one of two, `[ swap drop ]`.

shuffles#: < quote [ length 0 eq? ] ? >. drop true < bool >. ;
shuffles#: < quote >. dup first dup dup' eq? over drop' eq? or over swap' eq? or swap '.' eq? or swap rest shuffles# and < bool >. ;
picks#: < quote [ shuffles# not ] ? >. drop false < bool >. ;
picks#: < quote >. effect# over -+ -1 eq? swap 3 lt? and < bool >. ;

-- `cs choice#`: what a word's two clauses choose between two values,
-- `least'` if the greater, the first testing which is by `gt` or `lt`, as
-- `max`'s do, `greatest'` if the lesser, as `min`'s, and `none'` if
-- neither. Each clause must pick one of the two, which applying it to two
-- symbols shows.

chosen#: < gt' left' right' >. drop drop drop least' < sym >. ;
chosen#: < gt' right' left' >. drop drop drop greatest' < sym >. ;
chosen#: < lt' left' right' >. drop drop drop greatest' < sym >. ;
chosen#: < lt' right' left' >. drop drop drop least' < sym >. ;
chosen#: < a b c >. drop drop drop none' < sym >. ;

choice#: < quote [ length 2 eq? not ] ? >. drop none' < sym >. ;
choice#: < quote [ dup first first last '?' eq? swap last first last '?' eq? not and not ] ? >. drop none' < sym >. ;
choice#: < quote [ dup first last picks# swap last last picks# and not ] ? >. drop none' < sym >. ;
choice#: < quote >. dup first first -2 at swap dup first last left' right' rot . swap last last left' right' rot . chosen# < sym >. ;

-- `w ins f end#`: the end of the type's order that the choice w makes
-- has, f being `least'` or `greatest'`.

end#: < sym quote least' [ swap first swap stated# swap drop ] ? >. drop swap drop first least ;
end#: < sym quote greatest' [ swap first swap stated# swap drop ] ? >. drop swap drop first greatest ;
end#: < sym quote least' >. drop first dup dup "`\_` at \_ keeps the greater of two, and `least` gives \_ none: a clause of `least` would, `core.least: < \_' >. drop … ;`" ( _ no-word' ) fail ;
end#: < sym quote greatest' >. drop first dup dup "`\_` at \_ keeps the lesser of two, and `greatest` gives \_ none: a clause of `greatest` would, `core.greatest: < \_' >. drop … ;`" ( _ no-word' ) fail ;

-- How to name what has an identity: a word by its name, `"`max`"`, and
-- other code as a quotation.

named#: < sym >. "`\_`" < string >. ;
named#: < quote >. drop "the quotation" < string >. ;

no-identity#: < string quote >. first dup "\_ has no identity at \_ that its primitives give: at \_ it applies no one operation whose primitive `unit` knows, and chooses no greater or lesser" ( _ no-word' ) fail ;

-- `u ins identity#`, reading the words as data (6): `u` is an identity,
-- its operation as code, `[ + ]`, and `ins` the types it is for, `[ i64
-- i64 ]`. A word has the identity `unit` states for it, as a primitive
-- does; otherwise, with one clause for them, its code's; and with two
-- that keep one of the two by their order, the type's least value if they
-- keep the greater, as `max`'s do, its greatest if the lesser, as
-- `min`'s. Code that applies one word has the word's identity, and code
-- that swaps the two values first has the same, `[ swap + ]`. So `and^0`
-- is true at bool, `and` being `min`, and `max^0` the least i64. Any other
-- word has none, `-+` and `avg` among them. While it looks, code is held
-- with how to name it. The search's words end in `#`, being March's own
-- (17), so a program keeps the plain names.

identity#: < unit# quote >. [ quote ! ] dip identity# ;
identity#: < quote quote [ drop dup length 2 eq? swap last '.' eq? and ] ? >. over first named# swap identity# ;
identity#: < quote quote >. "the quotation" swap identity# ;
identity#: < quote string quote [ drop drop dup length 2 eq? swap last '.' eq? and ] ? >. [ [ first ] dip ] dip identity# ;
identity#: < quote string quote [ drop drop length 3 lt? ] ? >. rot drop no-identity# ;
identity#: < quote string quote [ drop drop dup first swap' eq? swap 2 at '.' eq? and ] ? >. [ [ 3 -1 slice ] dip ] dip identity# ;
identity#: < quote string quote >. rot drop no-identity# ;
identity#: < sym string quote [ drop drop unit' stated# ] ? >. drop drop unit ;
identity#: < sym string quote [ swap drop clauses# length 0 eq? ] ? >. first rot dup "\_ at \_ applies `\_`, for which `unit` gives no identity: a clause of `unit` would, `core.unit: < \_' >. drop … ;`" ( _ no-word' ) fail ;
identity#: < sym string quote [ swap drop clauses# length 1 eq? ] ? >. swap drop over over clauses# first last rot named# rot identity# ;
identity#: < sym string quote [ swap drop clauses# choice# none' eq? not ] ? >. swap drop over over clauses# choice# end# ;
identity#: < sym string quote >. swap drop swap named# swap no-identity# ;

-- A type converts an identity to itself by the fact that holds for two
-- values of it, which `identity#` finds.

i64: < unit# >. [ i64 i64 ] identity# < i64 >. ;
f64: < unit# >. [ f64 f64 ] identity# < f64 >. ;
money: < unit# >. [ money money ] identity# < money >. ;
string: < unit# >. [ string string ] identity# < string >. ;
bool: < unit# >. [ bool bool ] identity# < bool >. ;
int#: < unit# >. [ int# int# ] identity# < int# >. ;
dec#: < unit# >. [ dec# dec# ] identity# < dec# >. ;
ratio#: < unit# >. [ ratio# ratio# ] identity# < ratio# >. ;

-- Loops. `each` applies a quotation to each element, from the first, and
-- `each-right` from the last; the quotation takes its element and keeps the
-- values below it, as an accumulator. A fold starts from a value: `fold`
-- combines from the left, ((x e0) e1) e2, and `fold-right` from the right,
-- e0 (e1 (e2 x)), its quotation taking an element under the accumulator.
-- A reduction starts from the first element, or the last.

fold: rot swap each ;
fold-right: rot swap [ swap ] swap compose each-right ;
reduce: swap dup rest swap first rot fold ;
reduce-right: swap dup most swap last rot fold-right ;

-- `xs q scan`: the running reductions, from the left, as `reduce` gives
-- the last of them: `( 1 2 3 ) +' scan` is ( 1 3 6 ). A comprehension: each
-- turn reads the last value collected.

scan: ( _ dup first swap rest [ over swap ] _ compose each ) ;

-- `xs q within`: q run with the array's last elements as its stack, as many
-- as it takes, and what it leaves put in their place: `( 1 2 3 ) [ ~ ]
-- within` is `( 1 3 2 )`. `spread` puts an array's elements on the stack:
-- a vec's each a value, and any array's, inside an array literal, a run.

spread: < n a vec >. vec-spread ;
spread: < tuple >. tuple-spread ;
spread: < a ary >. [ ] each ;

-- `n q repeat` runs q n times.

repeat: swap range swap [ drop ] swap compose each ;

-- Values as text. `show` writes a value as the display does, so it reads
-- back: a string quoted, an array in parentheses. `>string` is a string's
-- own text, and any other value shown; a string's holes write values in
-- with it (march7/docs/STRINGS.md).

show: < string >. string-show < string >. ;
show: < i64 >. i64>text < string >. ;
show: < f64 >. f64>text < string >. ;
show: < money >. money>text < string >. ;
show: < nil >. drop "nil" < string >. ;
show: < 1 bool! >. drop "true" < string >. ;
show: < bool >. drop "false" < string >. ;
show: < sym >. sym-show < string >. ;
show: < tuple >. [ show ] map "(" swap [ " " swap concat concat ] each " )" concat < string >. ;
show: < a ary >. "(" swap [ show " " swap concat concat ] each " )" concat < string >. ;
show: < k v map >.
  "{" swap dup keys sort
  [ over over at show swap show " " swap concat " " concat swap concat
    rot swap concat swap ] each
  drop " }" concat < string >. ;

>string: < string >. < string >. ;
>string: < a >. show < string >. ;

-- Writing.

print: < string >. write ;
print: < a >. >string write ;
